AFBC seek/reset regression
=========================

Run on a Linux host with Python 3 and a C compiler:

    python3 tools/testing/amlogic/test_afbc_scanout.py

To also prove regression sensitivity with deliberately broken source variants:

    python3 tools/testing/amlogic/test_afbc_scanout.py --negative-controls

CC may select another compiler or add flags, for example:

    CC='cc -fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all' \
        python3 tools/testing/amlogic/test_afbc_scanout.py --negative-controls

No kernel build, device connection, video file, capture or image analysis is
needed. Generated sources and executables use an automatically removed
temporary directory. The working tree and production files are not changed.

The test compiles complete functions from the current video.c/video_keeper.c,
including both unregister callbacks, the recycle helper, AFBC lookup and the
keeper chain. Only their kernel, register and codec-memory boundaries are
simulated; it does not reimplement the fix. With hardware still reading the
previous AFBC header, releasing provider ownership must not allow the body,
header or double-write allocation to be reused before a modeled RDMA handoff.
The replacement remains retained, and retired allocations eventually release.

Cases cover full/light unregister, contiguous/scatter allocation types,
initialized per-layer registers, sparse/mixed deferred queues, RDMA compiled
in/out and disabled at runtime, lookup eligibility, blackout/no-keep policy,
already-current scanout, and failed-pin diagnostics. With --negative-controls,
twelve deliberately broken in-memory variants must compile but fail the
specific expected runtime assertions, including missing pins, late lookup,
reversed keeper order and immediate release. Only this optional sensitivity
check uses exact source-replacement anchors; update them after code refactors.

This is a deterministic ownership/order regression, not hardware acceptance:
the modeled 40 ms handoff does not prove that the existing 120 ms delay covers
every real interleaving. Provider descriptors/handles are assumed valid until
the unregister callback returns. Real SMP/IRQ/RDMA behavior and native Dolby
Vision enhancement-layer lifetime still require separate validation.
